module.exports = {
  title: 'Components & Communication',
  slug: 'components-communication',
  order: 1,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Props: Passing Data Down',
      slug: 'props',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Props are how data enters a component, and the rules around them encode Vue's most important architectural principle: <strong>one-way data flow</strong>. Parent state flows down; children never write back directly. Interviewers ask about props not because declaring one is hard, but because the follow-ups — "what happens if you mutate a prop?", "why does mutating a prop <em>object</em> not warn?", "how do you handle a prop that must be editable?" — reveal whether you understand why the restriction exists at all.</p>`,
        `<p>Get this wrong in production and you get the worst class of UI bug: state that changes for reasons nobody can trace. When any child can write to any parent's data, the question "what changed this value?" has no bounded answer. One-way flow makes it answerable, and props are where that discipline is enforced.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>prop</strong> is a custom attribute a parent sets on a child component. The child declares which props it accepts; the parent passes values; Vue makes those values available inside the child as read-only reactive data.</p>`,
        `<p>Three rules govern them, and together they are the whole model:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><strong>Props must be declared.</strong> An undeclared attribute is not a prop — it becomes a <em>fallthrough attribute</em> and lands on the child's root element instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Props are read-only in the child.</strong> Assigning to one warns in development. The parent owns the value.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Props are reactive.</strong> When the parent's value changes, the child re-renders. You never wire anything up for this.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `{{fig:ch2-one-way-data-flow.svg|Diagram showing props flowing down from parent to child components and events flowing back up, with a crossed-out arrow showing a child writing directly to parent state|Props down, events up. The child never writes to the parent's state — that constraint is what makes data flow traceable.}}`,
        `<p>The naming convention trips people up constantly: declare props in <strong>camelCase</strong> in JavaScript, pass them in <strong>kebab-case</strong> in templates. Vue bridges the two automatically, because HTML attribute names are case-insensitive — <code>userName</code> in an in-DOM template would arrive as <code>username</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Declaration and validation.</strong> The object form of <code>defineProps</code> lets you specify a type, whether it is required, a default, and a custom validator. In development Vue checks each of these and warns on violation; in production the checks are stripped, so validation is a development-time contract, not a runtime guarantee.</p>`,
        `<p>Defaults have one rule worth memorising: for <strong>objects and arrays</strong>, <code>default</code> must be a <em>factory function</em>. If it were a literal, every instance of the component would share the same object reference, and one instance mutating it would change all of them — the same reason <code>data</code> is a function.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Boolean casting</strong> is a special case that makes Vue feel like HTML. If a prop is declared <code>Boolean</code>, the bare attribute <code>&lt;Modal open&gt;</code> becomes <code>true</code>, and an absent attribute becomes <code>false</code> rather than <code>undefined</code>. That is why declaring a boolean prop's type matters even when you are not otherwise validating.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why mutation is forbidden — and the object loophole.</strong> Assigning to a prop breaks the ownership model: the parent re-renders, passes its value down again, and your change is silently overwritten. So Vue warns. But the check is on <em>assignment to the prop binding</em>, not on the object it points to. Passing an object gives the child a reference to the parent's object, and <code>props.user.name = 'x'</code> mutates the parent's data with no warning at all. It works — which is precisely what makes it dangerous. The data changed, the parent has no idea why, and the trail is cold.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>What to do when a prop needs to be editable.</strong> Three legitimate patterns, in order of preference:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>The value belongs to the parent → emit an event and let the parent update it (<code>v-model</code> is sugar over exactly this).</li>`,
        `<li>The prop is just an initial value → copy it into local state once with <code>ref(props.initial)</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>You need a transformed view of it → derive it with <code>computed</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Fallthrough attributes</strong> are the other half of the story. Any attribute the parent sets that is <em>not</em> a declared prop — <code>class</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>data-*</code>, <code>@click</code> — is applied automatically to the child's root element. <code>class</code> and <code>style</code> merge with the child's own; everything else is set directly. With multiple root elements Vue cannot guess where they belong, so it warns and drops them unless you bind <code>v-bind="$attrs"</code> explicitly. Setting <code>inheritAttrs: false</code> turns off the automatic behaviour when you need attributes on an inner element — a text input wrapped in a label, say.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- UserCard.vue — full prop declaration --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst props = defineProps({\n  // shorthand: type only\n  title: String,\n\n  // full form\n  user: {\n    type: Object,\n    required: true,\n  },\n  count: {\n    type: Number,\n    default: 0,\n  },\n  // objects/arrays MUST use a factory, or all instances share one object\n  tags: {\n    type: Array,\n    default: () =&gt; [],\n  },\n  // multiple accepted types\n  id: [String, Number],\n  // custom validation (dev-only)\n  variant: {\n    type: String,\n    default: 'primary',\n    validator: (v) =&gt; ['primary', 'secondary', 'danger'].includes(v),\n  },\n  // Boolean gets HTML-like casting: &lt;UserCard disabled&gt; → true\n  disabled: {\n    type: Boolean,\n    default: false,\n  },\n})\n\n// props is reactive — use it in computeds and watchers\nconst displayName = computed(() =&gt; props.user.name ?? 'Anonymous')\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Parent.vue — camelCase in JS, kebab-case in template --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;UserCard\n    :user="currentUser"\n    :count="42"\n    title="Team lead"          &lt;!-- static string, no colon needed --&gt;\n    :id="user.id"\n    variant="danger"\n    disabled                    &lt;!-- Boolean shorthand → true --&gt;\n    class="mt-4"                &lt;!-- not a prop → falls through to root --&gt;\n  /&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- Spread an object as props --&gt;\n  &lt;UserCard v-bind="cardProps" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>TypeScript gives you type-only declaration, which is the nicest form when you have TS available:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup lang="ts"&gt;\ninterface User { id: number; name: string; role?: string }\n\n// types drive both inference and dev-time checks\nconst props = withDefaults(\n  defineProps&lt;{\n    user: User\n    count?: number\n    tags?: string[]\n  }&gt;(),\n  {\n    count: 0,\n    tags: () =&gt; [],   // still a factory for arrays/objects\n  },\n)\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The mutation rules, made concrete:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const props = defineProps({ modelValue: String, user: Object })\n\n// ❌ Warns: "Attempting to mutate prop"\nprops.modelValue = 'new'\n\n// ❌ NO warning — and that's the danger. Mutates the parent's object directly.\nprops.user.name = 'Changed'\n\n// ✅ 1. It's the parent's state → tell the parent\nconst emit = defineEmits(['update:modelValue'])\nemit('update:modelValue', 'new')\n\n// ✅ 2. It's only a starting value → copy once into local state\nconst draft = ref(props.modelValue)\n\n// ✅ 3. You need a derived view → compute it\nconst upper = computed(() =&gt; props.modelValue.toUpperCase())\n\n// ✅ 4. Local copy that RESETS when the prop changes\nwatch(() =&gt; props.user, (u) =&gt; { draft.value = { ...u } }, { deep: true })</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Fallthrough attributes and when to switch them off:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- BaseInput.vue — attributes should land on the &lt;input&gt;, not the wrapper --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\ndefineOptions({ inheritAttrs: false })   // stop the automatic root binding\ndefineProps({ label: String })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;label class="field"&gt;\n    &lt;span&gt;{{ label }}&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;!-- placeholder, type, @focus, aria-* … all land here --&gt;\n    &lt;input v-bind="$attrs"&gt;\n  &lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Used as: &lt;BaseInput label="Email" type="email" placeholder="you@x.com" required /&gt; --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating an object or array prop.</strong> No warning fires, so it feels legal. It writes straight into the parent's state and destroys traceability. This is the single most important gotcha in this topic.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>A literal default for an object or array.</strong> <code>default: []</code> shares one array across every instance of the component. Always <code>default: () =&gt; []</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>:</code> when passing a number or boolean.</strong> <code>count="42"</code> passes the <em>string</em> <code>"42"</code>; <code>:count="42"</code> passes the number. A silent source of <code>"421"</code> instead of <code>43</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Destructuring props and losing reactivity.</strong> <code>const { count } = defineProps(...)</code> copies the value out. (Vue 3.5+ can compile-transform this in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, but the safe habit is to keep the <code>props.</code> prefix or use <code>toRefs</code>.)</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not declaring a prop you use.</strong> It silently becomes a fallthrough attribute and appears in the DOM as a stray attribute rather than reaching your script.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting validators to run in production.</strong> They are stripped from the production build. Validate real input at the API boundary, not in prop validators.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Missing fallthrough attributes with multiple root elements.</strong> Vue cannot choose a target, so it warns and drops them; bind <code>$attrs</code> explicitly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Declaring a boolean prop without <code>type: Boolean</code>.</strong> You lose the bare-attribute casting, and <code>&lt;Modal open&gt;</code> arrives as an empty string.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Declare every prop with a type, and add <code>required</code> or a sensible <code>default</code> — the declaration is the component's public API and its documentation.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer many small primitive props over one big object prop: it makes the contract explicit, keeps re-renders narrow and removes the temptation to mutate.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat props as immutable without exception. If you need to change a value, emit; if you need a starting point, copy; if you need a variation, compute.</li>`,
        `<li>Use TypeScript's <code>defineProps&lt;T&gt;()</code> with <code>withDefaults</code> when TS is available — real compile-time checking beats development-only warnings.</li>`,
        `<li>Name props for what they mean to the child, not where they came from: <code>disabled</code>, not <code>parentSaysDisabled</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>inheritAttrs: false</code> plus explicit <code>v-bind="$attrs"</code> in any wrapper component, so consumers can pass native attributes to the element that should receive them.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep validators cheap and pure — they run on every update in development.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why can't a child mutate a prop?"</strong><br>A: Because it breaks one-way data flow. The parent owns the value; if the child writes to it, the next parent re-render passes the original value down again and silently overwrites the change — and more importantly, nobody can answer "what changed this?" any more. Vue warns on direct assignment. The correct moves are to emit an event so the owner updates it, copy it into local state if it is only an initial value, or derive a computed if you need a transformed view.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Then why does <code>props.user.name = 'x'</code> not warn?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because the check is on assignment to the prop binding, not on the object it references. Objects are passed by reference, so the child holds the parent's actual object and mutating it works — and mutates the parent's state with no warning. It is the more dangerous version of the same mistake precisely because nothing tells you. Pass primitives where you can, and emit instead of mutating.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must object and array prop defaults be factory functions?"</strong><br>A: The default is evaluated per component instance, but a literal object would be created once when the component is <em>defined</em>, so every instance would share the same reference and one instance's mutation would leak into all of them. A factory produces a fresh value per instance. It is the same reason <code>data</code> is a function in the Options API.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What are fallthrough attributes?"</strong><br>A: Attributes and listeners the parent sets that aren't declared props. Vue applies them automatically to the child's single root element — <code>class</code> and <code>style</code> merge with the child's own, everything else is set directly. With multiple root elements Vue can't pick a target, warns, and drops them. Set <code>inheritAttrs: false</code> and bind <code>v-bind="$attrs"</code> to place them on an inner element, which is what wrapper components around native inputs should do.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A prop should be editable inside the child — a modal's open state, say. How?"</strong><br>A: Keep ownership with the parent and use <code>v-model</code>: declare a <code>modelValue</code> prop and emit <code>update:modelValue</code>. The parent stays the single source of truth and the child stays controlled. If the value is genuinely local after the first render — a form draft seeded from a prop — copy it into a <code>ref</code> once, and add a watcher if it should reset when the prop changes.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Do prop validators run in production?"</strong><br>A: No — type checks and validators are development-only and stripped from the production build, along with the warnings. They are a contract for developers, not runtime input validation. Anything coming from a user or an API needs real validation at that boundary.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Props: declared, read-only, reactive. camelCase in JS ↔ kebab-case in templates.</li>`,
        `<li><code>defineProps</code> — compiler macro, never imported. TS form: <code>defineProps&lt;T&gt;()</code> + <code>withDefaults</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Object/array defaults <strong>must</strong> be factories: <code>default: () =&gt; []</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>type: Boolean</code> enables bare-attribute casting: <code>&lt;Modal open&gt;</code> → <code>true</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Static <code>title="x"</code> = string; <code>:count="42"</code> = number. Forgetting <code>:</code> passes a string.</li>`,
        `<li>Assigning to a prop → warns. Mutating a prop <strong>object</strong> → no warning, still wrong.</li>`,
        `<li>Editable value: emit (<code>v-model</code>) · copy to <code>ref</code> · derive with <code>computed</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Undeclared attributes → fallthrough to root; <code>class</code>/<code>style</code> merge. <code>inheritAttrs: false</code> + <code>v-bind="$attrs"</code> to redirect.</li>`,
        `<li>Validators and type checks are <strong>dev-only</strong>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Emits: Sending Events Up',
      slug: 'emits-events',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>If props are how data flows down, emits are how intent flows back up. Together they are Vue's complete answer to parent–child communication, and interviewers treat the pair as one question. What they are really testing with emits is whether you understand <em>who owns state</em>: a well-designed child announces that something happened and lets the owner decide what it means, rather than reaching up and changing things itself.</p>`,
        `<p>Emits are also the mechanism underneath <code>v-model</code> on components, which is the single most-used Vue pattern after props. You cannot build a decent form component library without understanding them precisely.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A component <strong>emits</strong> a custom event; the parent listens for it with <code>@event-name</code>. The child says "the user selected item 42"; the parent decides whether that means navigating, filtering or saving. The child does not know and should not care.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Child --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst emit = defineEmits(['select'])\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;button @click="emit('select', 42)"&gt;Pick&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Parent --&gt;\n&lt;ItemButton @select="handleSelect" /&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Declaring emits with <code>defineEmits</code> is optional at runtime but strongly recommended, for a reason that is not obvious: <strong>declared emits are removed from <code>$attrs</code></strong>. An undeclared <code>@select</code> listener falls through to the child's root element as a native DOM listener — so a native <code>click</code> and an emitted <code>click</code> can both fire, and your handler runs twice. Declaring the event is what prevents that.</p>`,
        `<p>Event names use <strong>kebab-case</strong> in templates and may be written either way in <code>emit()</code>, though kebab-case in both is the convention that avoids surprises.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><code>emit(name, ...args)</code> looks up the listener the parent passed as a prop named <code>onEventName</code> — that is literally how Vue implements it — and calls it with the arguments. There is no event bus, no bubbling, no propagation through intermediate components. An event is a direct function call from child to its immediate parent, which is why you cannot listen for a grandchild's event on a grandparent without forwarding it explicitly.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch2-emit-flow.svg|Sequence diagram of a child calling emit with a payload, Vue resolving the onEventName prop, and the parent handler updating state which flows back down as props|emit() is a direct call to the listener the parent passed down — no bubbling, no event bus.}}`,
        `<p><strong><code>v-model</code> on a component</strong> is pure sugar over this mechanism, and being able to expand it by hand is a standard interview request:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- These two are identical --&gt;\n&lt;CustomInput v-model="text" /&gt;\n&lt;CustomInput :model-value="text" @update:model-value="text = $event" /&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>So a <code>v-model</code>-compatible component declares a <code>modelValue</code> prop and an <code>update:modelValue</code> emit. Multiple <code>v-model</code>s on one component are supported via named arguments — <code>v-model:title</code> pairs with the <code>title</code> prop and <code>update:title</code> event — which is how a date-range picker exposes <code>v-model:start</code> and <code>v-model:end</code>.</p>`,
        `<p>Vue 3.4 added <code>defineModel()</code>, which collapses the whole pattern into one line and is now the recommended form: it declares the prop and the emit for you and hands back a writable ref.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Validation.</strong> The object form of <code>defineEmits</code> lets you attach a validator per event, returning a boolean. Like prop validators it is development-only. Its real value is documentation — the declaration states the payload shape at the top of the file.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- SearchBox.vue --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\n\n// array form — simple declaration\nconst emit = defineEmits(['search', 'clear'])\n\nconst query = ref('')\n\nfunction submit() {\n  if (!query.value.trim()) return\n  emit('search', { term: query.value, at: Date.now() })  // payload can be anything\n}\nfunction clear() {\n  query.value = ''\n  emit('clear')                                          // no payload is fine\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;form @submit.prevent="submit"&gt;\n    &lt;input v-model="query"&gt;\n    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Search&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;button type="button" @click="clear"&gt;Clear&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Parent — $event is the payload; use an inline handler for extra args --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;SearchBox @search="onSearch" @clear="results = []" /&gt;\n  &lt;SearchBox @search="onSearch($event, 'secondary')" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nfunction onSearch({ term }) { fetchResults(term) }\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Object form with validators, and the TypeScript equivalent:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Runtime validation — dev only, but documents the payload\nconst emit = defineEmits({\n  search: (payload) =&gt; typeof payload?.term === 'string',\n  clear: null,                       // null = no validation\n  'update:page': (n) =&gt; Number.isInteger(n) &amp;&amp; n &gt; 0,\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-typescript">// TS — real compile-time checking of names AND payloads\nconst emit = defineEmits&lt;{\n  search: [payload: { term: string; at: number }]\n  clear: []\n  'update:page': [page: number]\n}&gt;()</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>v-model</code> on a component, three ways — old, explicit and modern:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ① The manual pattern (Vue 3.0–3.3) --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\ndefineProps({ modelValue: String })\nconst emit = defineEmits(['update:modelValue'])\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input :value="modelValue" @input="emit('update:modelValue', $event.target.value)"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ② Vue 3.4+ — defineModel does both halves --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst model = defineModel()          // writable ref, two-way wired\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="model"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- ③ Multiple named models --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst start = defineModel('start')\nconst end   = defineModel('end')\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;!-- Parent: &lt;DateRange v-model:start="from" v-model:end="to" /&gt; --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Forwarding an event through an intermediate component — events do not bubble, so you must be explicit:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Middle.vue — a wrapper that must not swallow the child's event --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst emit = defineEmits(['select'])\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- explicit re-emit --&gt;\n  &lt;ItemList @select="emit('select', $event)" /&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- or forward every listener at once --&gt;\n  &lt;ItemList v-bind="$attrs" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Not declaring emits, then getting double handlers.</strong> An undeclared <code>@click</code> falls through to the root element as a native listener <em>and</em> fires your emit — the handler runs twice. Declaring the event removes it from <code>$attrs</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting events to bubble.</strong> They do not. A grandchild's event is invisible to a grandparent unless every layer re-emits it — or you use <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> or a store instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Emitting a mutation instead of an intent.</strong> <code>emit('setUserName', 'x')</code> makes the child dictate the parent's implementation. <code>emit('name-change', 'x')</code> reports what happened and leaves the decision upstream.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Name-case mismatches.</strong> <code>emit('updateItem')</code> listened to as <code>@update-item</code> does not match in all cases. Use kebab-case consistently on both sides.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.prevent</code> on form submits.</strong> The page reloads and the emit never reaches the parent — a genuinely confusing five minutes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Passing the raw DOM event as a payload.</strong> Emit meaning (<code>{ id: 42 }</code>), not plumbing (a <code>MouseEvent</code>); the parent should not have to know your internal markup.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for an event bus.</strong> Vue 3 removed <code>$on</code>/<code>$off</code> deliberately — global buses make data flow untraceable. Use props/emits, provide/inject, or Pinia.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming emit validators run in production.</strong> They do not, exactly like prop validators.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Always declare emits with <code>defineEmits</code> — for the <code>$attrs</code> behaviour, for documentation, and for TS checking.</li>`,
        `<li>Name events after what happened in the child's own terms (<code>submit</code>, <code>close</code>, <code>select</code>), not after what the parent should do.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>defineModel()</code> for two-way bindings on Vue 3.4+; fall back to the explicit <code>modelValue</code>/<code>update:modelValue</code> pair on older versions.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep payloads small and serialisable. Emit an id, not a whole reactive object the parent might mutate.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the TypeScript call-signature form of <code>defineEmits</code> — it catches typo'd event names and wrong payloads at compile time.</li>`,
        `<li>When more than two layers need to communicate, stop forwarding events and reach for <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> or a Pinia store — chains of re-emits are a design smell.</li>`,
        `<li>Document each event's payload next to its declaration; that block is the component's public API.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do child and parent communicate in Vue?"</strong><br>A: Props down, events up. The parent passes data as props, which the child treats as read-only; the child reports what happened with <code>emit()</code> and the parent decides how to respond. That keeps a single owner for every piece of state. For non-adjacent components, <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> covers deep trees and Pinia covers genuinely shared application state.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why declare emits if it works without declaring them?"</strong><br>A: Three reasons, one of them a real bug. Declared events are removed from <code>$attrs</code>, so they don't also fall through to the root element as native listeners — undeclared <code>@click</code> gives you a handler that fires twice. Beyond that, the declaration documents the component's API and, with the TypeScript form, gives compile-time checking of event names and payloads.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Expand <code>v-model</code> on a custom component."</strong><br>A: <code>&lt;Comp v-model="x" /&gt;</code> compiles to <code>&lt;Comp :model-value="x" @update:model-value="x = $event" /&gt;</code>. So the component declares a <code>modelValue</code> prop and an <code>update:modelValue</code> emit. Named models — <code>v-model:title</code> — use the <code>title</code> prop and <code>update:title</code> event, which is how a component exposes several two-way bindings. From 3.4, <code>defineModel()</code> declares both halves and returns a writable ref.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Do emitted events bubble like DOM events?"</strong><br>A: No. <code>emit()</code> resolves the <code>onEventName</code> prop the parent passed and calls it directly — there is no propagation through the tree. A grandparent cannot hear a grandchild unless each layer re-emits. If you find yourself forwarding through several layers, that is the signal to switch to provide/inject or a store.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Vue 2 had an event bus with <code>$on</code>. Why was it removed?"</strong><br>A: Because a global bus makes data flow untraceable: any component can emit anything and any component can listen, so "what triggered this?" has no bounded answer, and listeners leak when components unmount without cleanup. Vue 3 removed <code>$on</code>/<code>$off</code>/<code>$once</code> and points you at explicit props/emits for adjacent components, provide/inject for deep trees, and Pinia for shared state.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>const emit = defineEmits([...])</code> → <code>emit('name', payload)</code>; parent listens with <code>@name</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Declaring emits removes them from <code>$attrs</code> ⇒ prevents double-firing native listeners.</li>`,
        `<li>No bubbling — <code>emit</code> is a direct call to the <code>onEventName</code> prop.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-model</code> ≡ <code>:model-value</code> + <code>@update:model-value</code>. Named: <code>v-model:title</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 3.4+: <code>const model = defineModel()</code> — one line, writable ref.</li>`,
        `<li>TS form: <code>defineEmits&lt;{ search: [q: string] }&gt;()</code> for checked names and payloads.</li>`,
        `<li>Emit <strong>intent</strong> (<code>select</code>), not commands (<code>setParentValue</code>).</li>`,
        `<li>kebab-case event names on both sides; validators are dev-only.</li>`,
        `<li>No event bus in Vue 3 — use provide/inject or Pinia for distant components.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Slots: Content Projection',
      slug: 'slots',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Props let a parent configure a child with <em>data</em>. Slots let a parent supply <em>markup</em>. That difference is what separates a component you can use in three places from one you can use in thirty. Every serious component library — Vuetify, PrimeVue, Headless UI — is built on slots, and interviewers ask about them to find out whether you have designed a reusable component or only consumed one.</p>`,
        `<p>Scoped slots in particular are the idea people struggle to articulate. They invert the usual relationship: the child controls the logic and the data, the parent controls the rendering. Being able to explain that inversion clearly, with an example, is a strong signal in any Vue interview.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> is a placeholder in a child's template that the parent fills with content. Anything between the component's opening and closing tags is projected into it.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Card.vue --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;&lt;div class="card"&gt;&lt;slot&gt;&lt;/slot&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Usage: everything inside lands in the slot --&gt;\n&lt;Card&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Title&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Body&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/Card&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>There are three flavours, and they answer three different questions:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Default slot</strong> — "put your content here."</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Named slots</strong> — "put your content in one of <em>these</em> places" (header, body, footer).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Scoped slots</strong> — "put your content here, and here is the data you'll need to render it."</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch2-slots.svg|Diagram showing a parent projecting content into a child's default, named header and footer slots, and a scoped slot passing an item back up to the parent's template|Slots project markup down; scoped slots also pass data back up so the parent can decide how to render it.}}`,
        `<p>The mental model that makes everything else click: <strong>slot content is compiled in the parent's scope.</strong> The parent writes it, so it can only see the parent's data. A scoped slot is the child's way of lending the parent something it otherwise could not reach.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>At compile time, slot content becomes a <strong>function</strong> — one per slot — collected into a <code>$slots</code> object on the child. Rendering <code>&lt;slot /&gt;</code> calls that function and inserts the resulting vnodes. Once you see slots as functions, three otherwise-mysterious behaviours become obvious:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Scope.</strong> The function closes over the <em>parent's</em> setup scope, which is exactly why slot content cannot see the child's variables.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Scoped slots pass arguments.</strong> <code>&lt;slot :item="row" /&gt;</code> calls the parent's function with <code>{ item: row }</code>. <code>v-slot="props"</code> names that parameter. It is a callback, plain and simple.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>$slots</code> is checkable.</strong> <code>v-if="$slots.footer"</code> lets a component render a wrapper only when the parent actually supplied that slot — the standard way to avoid empty bordered footers.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Fallback content</strong> is whatever sits between <code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/slot&gt;</code>; it renders when the parent supplies nothing. That is how you give a component sensible defaults while keeping it overridable.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Syntax rules</strong> worth committing to memory: <code>v-slot</code> only goes on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> tags or on the component itself; its shorthand is <code>#</code>; and unnamed content goes to the slot named <code>default</code>. Slot names can be dynamic — <code>#[name]</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Renderless components</strong> are the logical extreme: a component with no markup at all that exposes only behaviour through a scoped slot. In modern Vue, composables have largely replaced them — a composable does the same job with less ceremony and better TypeScript. Knowing that trade-off is a good senior-level answer.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- BaseCard.vue — named slots with fallbacks and conditional wrappers --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;article class="card"&gt;\n    &lt;header v-if="$slots.header" class="card__header"&gt;\n      &lt;slot name="header"&gt;&lt;/slot&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n\n    &lt;div class="card__body"&gt;\n      &lt;slot&gt;Nothing to show yet.&lt;/slot&gt;   &lt;!-- fallback content --&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n\n    &lt;!-- footer chrome only exists if the parent filled it --&gt;\n    &lt;footer v-if="$slots.footer" class="card__footer"&gt;\n      &lt;slot name="footer"&gt;&lt;/slot&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  &lt;/article&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Using it --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;BaseCard&gt;\n    &lt;template #header&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;{{ user.name }}&lt;/h2&gt;   &lt;!-- parent's data — parent's scope --&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n\n    &lt;p&gt;Implicit default slot content.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n    &lt;template #footer&gt;\n      &lt;button @click="save"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/BaseCard&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong>Scoped slots</strong> — the child owns fetching, sorting and pagination; the parent owns appearance:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- DataTable.vue — logic lives here --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst props = defineProps({ rows: Array, columns: Array })\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;table&gt;\n    &lt;thead&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;\n        &lt;th v-for="col in columns" :key="col.key"&gt;\n          &lt;!-- per-column header override, with a sensible default --&gt;\n          &lt;slot :name="\`header-\${col.key}\`" :column="col"&gt;{{ col.label }}&lt;/slot&gt;\n        &lt;/th&gt;\n      &lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/thead&gt;\n    &lt;tbody&gt;\n      &lt;tr v-for="(row, index) in rows" :key="row.id"&gt;\n        &lt;td v-for="col in columns" :key="col.key"&gt;\n          &lt;!-- hand the parent everything it needs to render this cell --&gt;\n          &lt;slot name="cell" :row="row" :column="col" :index="index" :value="row[col.key]"&gt;\n            {{ row[col.key] }}\n          &lt;/slot&gt;\n        &lt;/td&gt;\n      &lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/tbody&gt;\n  &lt;/table&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Parent decides how each cell looks, without DataTable knowing anything about it --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;DataTable :rows="users" :columns="cols"&gt;\n    &lt;template #cell="{ row, column, value }"&gt;\n      &lt;RouterLink v-if="column.key === 'name'" :to="\`/users/\${row.id}\`"&gt;{{ value }}&lt;/RouterLink&gt;\n      &lt;StatusBadge v-else-if="column.key === 'status'" :status="value" /&gt;\n      &lt;span v-else&gt;{{ value }}&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/DataTable&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Shorthand and destructuring forms you will meet in real codebases:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Full → shorthand --&gt;\n&lt;template v-slot:header&gt;…&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;template #header&gt;…&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Scoped: full → destructured → renamed → defaulted --&gt;\n&lt;template #cell="slotProps"&gt;{{ slotProps.row.name }}&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;template #cell="{ row }"&gt;{{ row.name }}&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;template #cell="{ row: person }"&gt;{{ person.name }}&lt;/template&gt;\n&lt;template #cell="{ row, index = 0 }"&gt;{{ index }}&lt;/template&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Only a default scoped slot? v-slot can go on the component itself --&gt;\n&lt;MouseTracker v-slot="{ x, y }"&gt;{{ x }}, {{ y }}&lt;/MouseTracker&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Dynamic slot name --&gt;\n&lt;template #[dynamicName]="props"&gt;…&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Accessing slots from script — for wrappers that must forward everything:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { useSlots, computed } from 'vue'\nconst slots = useSlots()\nconst hasFooter = computed(() =&gt; Boolean(slots.footer))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- forward every slot the parent gave us to an inner component --&gt;\n  &lt;InnerComponent&gt;\n    &lt;template v-for="(_, name) in $slots" #[name]="scope"&gt;\n      &lt;slot :name="name" v-bind="scope ?? {}" /&gt;\n    &lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/InnerComponent&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting slot content to see the child's data.</strong> It is compiled in the parent's scope. If the parent needs the child's state, the child must pass it out through a scoped slot.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting <code>v-slot</code> on a plain element.</strong> It belongs on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> or on the component tag; <code>&lt;div #header&gt;</code> is invalid.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Rendering empty chrome.</strong> A <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> with borders and padding around an unfilled slot shows as a stray strip. Guard with <code>v-if="$slots.footer"</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Whitespace counting as content.</strong> A slot containing only whitespace or an HTML comment can still make <code>$slots.x</code> truthy in some setups — check what you are actually testing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mixing implicit default content with named <code>&lt;template&gt;</code>s carelessly.</strong> It works, but readers lose track of what goes where; be explicit with <code>#default</code> once a component has several slots.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using Vue 2's <code>slot="x"</code> / <code>slot-scope</code>.</strong> Removed in Vue 3 — everything is <code>v-slot</code> / <code>#</code> now.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Deep slot forwarding chains.</strong> Passing slots through four wrappers is nearly unreadable; reconsider the composition instead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for renderless components out of habit.</strong> For pure logic reuse, a composable is simpler, better typed and cheaper — slots are for markup flexibility.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Provide fallback content for every slot so the component is useful with no configuration at all.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard optional structural wrappers with <code>v-if="$slots.name"</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer a scoped slot over a dozen configuration props once a component needs to render arbitrary content — the prop-per-variation approach does not scale.</li>`,
        `<li>Name slots for their position or role (<code>header</code>, <code>actions</code>, <code>empty-state</code>), not for what you expect to be inside them.</li>`,
        `<li>Expose the minimum data a scoped slot needs; a slot's props are as much a public API as anything else.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the <code>#</code> shorthand and destructure slot props — <code>#cell="{ row }"</code> reads far better than <code>slotProps.row</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for composables for logic reuse, and slots for markup flexibility. Use both together when a component genuinely owns behaviour and delegates presentation.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What are slots and why do they exist?"</strong><br>A: Slots are content projection — a placeholder in a child's template that the parent fills with markup. Props configure a component with data; slots let the parent supply structure. Without them, every visual variation becomes another prop and a component ends up with twenty booleans. There are default, named and scoped slots.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's a scoped slot and when would you use one?"</strong><br>A: A slot that passes data from the child back to the parent's slot content: the child renders <code>&lt;slot :row="row" /&gt;</code> and the parent receives it with <code>#default="{ row }"</code>. It inverts the usual split — the child owns logic (fetching, sorting, pagination), the parent owns rendering. A data table is the canonical example: the table handles the mechanics and the consumer decides that the name column is a link and the status column is a badge.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Which scope is slot content compiled in?" (trap)</strong><br>A: The parent's. Slot content is written in the parent's template, so it closes over the parent's setup scope and cannot see the child's variables. Under the hood each slot is a function defined in the parent and invoked by the child; a scoped slot is that function being called <em>with arguments</em>, which is how child data reaches parent markup.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you avoid rendering an empty footer when the slot isn't used?"</strong><br>A: Check <code>$slots</code>. <code>&lt;footer v-if="$slots.footer"&gt;</code> renders the wrapper only when the parent actually supplied that slot — otherwise you ship a bordered, padded strip containing nothing. From script, <code>useSlots()</code> gives the same object.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Renderless components vs composables?"</strong><br>A: A renderless component has no template of its own and exposes behaviour purely through a scoped slot — it was the Vue 2 way to share stateful logic. In Vue 3 a composable does the same job as a plain function: no component instance, no vnode overhead, better TypeScript inference and easier testing. I'd use a composable for logic reuse and keep slots for cases where the parent genuinely needs to control markup.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> = placeholder; content between the component's tags fills it.</li>`,
        `<li>Three kinds: <strong>default</strong> · <strong>named</strong> (<code>#header</code>) · <strong>scoped</strong> (<code>#row="{ item }"</code>).</li>`,
        `<li>Slot content compiles in the <strong>parent's</strong> scope — it can't see child state.</li>`,
        `<li>Slots are functions; scoped slots are functions called with arguments.</li>`,
        `<li>Fallback = content between <code>&lt;slot&gt;…&lt;/slot&gt;</code>, rendered when nothing is supplied.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-if="$slots.footer"</code> to skip empty wrappers; <code>useSlots()</code> in script.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-slot</code> → <code>#</code>; only on <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> or the component tag. Dynamic: <code>#[name]</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 2's <code>slot=</code> / <code>slot-scope</code> are gone.</li>`,
        `<li>Logic reuse → composable. Markup flexibility → slot.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'provide / inject: Passing Data Through the Tree',
      slug: 'provide-inject',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Props work beautifully between a parent and its child. They work badly between a component and its great-great-grandchild, because every layer in between has to declare and forward a prop it does not use. That is <strong>prop drilling</strong>, and <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> is Vue's built-in answer to it.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers like this topic because the interesting question is not the API — it is the judgement. When is prop drilling acceptable? When does provide/inject beat a store? What do you give up by using it? Those answers separate a developer who reaches for the nearest tool from one who picks deliberately.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>An ancestor <strong>provides</strong> a value under a key. Any descendant, at any depth, <strong>injects</strong> it by that key. Components in between neither know nor care.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Ancestor\nprovide('theme', 'dark')\n\n// Any descendant, however deep\nconst theme = inject('theme')</code></pre>`,
        `{{fig:ch2-provide-inject.svg|Comparison of prop drilling through four intermediate components versus provide at the top and inject at the bottom bypassing the middle layers|Prop drilling forces every intermediate component to forward data it doesn't use; provide/inject skips them entirely.}}`,
        `<p>The critical caveat, and the one interviewers push on: <strong>provided values are not reactive by default.</strong> Provide a primitive and the descendant gets a snapshot that never changes. To keep reactivity, provide a <code>ref</code>, a <code>reactive</code> object or a <code>computed</code> — the reactivity travels with the object, not with the mechanism.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Every component instance holds a <code>provides</code> object that prototypally inherits from its parent's. Calling <code>provide()</code> adds a key to the current component's own object. <code>inject()</code> walks that prototype chain upward, returning the first match — so the <strong>nearest</strong> provider wins, which lets a subtree deliberately override an ancestor's value (a dark-themed panel inside a light app).</p>`,
        `<p>Both functions must be called <strong>synchronously during <code>setup()</code></strong>. Calling <code>inject()</code> inside an event handler or after an <code>await</code> fails, because Vue resolves them against the "currently rendering instance," which only exists during setup. This is the same constraint that applies to lifecycle hooks, and the same rule that makes composables work.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Injection keys and collisions.</strong> String keys are global to the tree, so two libraries both using <code>'store'</code> collide silently. <code>Symbol</code> keys, exported from a shared module, make collisions impossible and give TypeScript something to hang types on via <code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Defaults.</strong> <code>inject('key', fallback)</code> returns the fallback when no provider exists, instead of <code>undefined</code> plus a development warning. For an object default, pass <code>true</code> as the third argument to have the fallback treated as a factory. A missing required injection should usually throw with a clear message rather than continue with <code>undefined</code>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Keeping writes controlled.</strong> The temptation is to provide a mutable <code>reactive</code> object and let every descendant write to it — which recreates the untraceable-mutation problem props were designed to prevent. The disciplined pattern is to provide <em>readonly</em> state plus explicit mutator functions, so changes still funnel through one place.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>App-level provide.</strong> <code>app.provide(key, value)</code> makes something available to every component in the app — useful for an API base URL or a configuration object, and the seam where you swap in a test double.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// keys.js — Symbol keys, typed, in one shared module\nimport type { InjectionKey, Ref } from 'vue'\n\nexport interface ThemeContext {\n  theme: Readonly&lt;Ref&lt;'light' | 'dark'&gt;&gt;\n  toggle: () =&gt; void\n}\n\nexport const themeKey = Symbol('theme') as InjectionKey&lt;ThemeContext&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- App.vue — provide reactive state + controlled mutators --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, readonly, provide } from 'vue'\nimport { themeKey } from './keys'\n\nconst theme = ref('light')\nfunction toggle() {\n  theme.value = theme.value === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light'\n}\n\n// readonly() stops descendants writing directly — they must call toggle()\nprovide(themeKey, { theme: readonly(theme), toggle })\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- DeeplyNestedButton.vue — five levels down, no props involved --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { inject } from 'vue'\nimport { themeKey } from './keys'\n\nconst ctx = inject(themeKey)\nif (!ctx) throw new Error('ThemeToggle must be used inside a theme provider')\nconst { theme, toggle } = ctx\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;button :class="\`btn--\${theme}\`" @click="toggle"&gt;\n    Theme: {{ theme }}\n  &lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The reactivity rule, demonstrated — this is the bug people hit:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Provides a snapshot. count is 0 in the descendant forever.\nlet count = 0\nprovide('count', count)\nsetInterval(() =&gt; count++, 1000)   // descendant never sees a change\n\n// ✅ Provide the ref itself — reactivity travels with the object\nconst count = ref(0)\nprovide('count', count)\nsetInterval(() =&gt; count.value++, 1000)\n\n// ✅ A computed works the same way\nprovide('doubled', computed(() =&gt; count.value * 2))\n\n// ⚠️ Also broken: unwrapping at provide time strips the reactivity again\nprovide('count', count.value)      // passes a number, not the ref</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Defaults, required injections and app-level provide:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// simple default\nconst locale = inject('locale', 'en')\n\n// factory default (third arg = treatDefaultAsFactory)\nconst config = inject('config', () =&gt; ({ retries: 3 }), true)\n\n// required — fail loudly rather than continue with undefined\nfunction injectStrict(key, message) {\n  const value = inject(key)\n  if (value === undefined) throw new Error(message ?? \`Missing provider for \${String(key)}\`)\n  return value\n}\n\n// main.js — available to every component in the app\napp.provide('apiBaseUrl', import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The idiomatic packaging: wrap provide/inject in a composable pair so consumers never touch keys:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// useTheme.js\nimport { ref, readonly, provide, inject } from 'vue'\nconst key = Symbol('theme')\n\nexport function provideTheme(initial = 'light') {\n  const theme = ref(initial)\n  const toggle = () =&gt; { theme.value = theme.value === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light' }\n  const ctx = { theme: readonly(theme), toggle }\n  provide(key, ctx)\n  return ctx                        // the provider can use it too\n}\n\nexport function useTheme() {\n  const ctx = inject(key)\n  if (!ctx) throw new Error('useTheme() requires provideTheme() in an ancestor')\n  return ctx\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Providing a plain value and expecting updates.</strong> Provide a <code>ref</code>, <code>reactive</code> or <code>computed</code>. Providing <code>count.value</code> instead of <code>count</code> is the same mistake in disguise.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>inject()</code> outside setup.</strong> Inside an event handler, a callback or after an <code>await</code>, there is no current instance and the injection resolves to <code>undefined</code>. Inject at the top of setup and use the value later.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>String keys that collide.</strong> <code>'store'</code> or <code>'config'</code> from two sources silently shadow each other. Use exported <code>Symbol</code>s.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Letting every descendant write to provided state.</strong> That is a global mutable variable with extra steps. Provide <code>readonly()</code> state plus mutator functions.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using it for genuinely global application state.</strong> Auth, cart, notifications belong in Pinia — you get devtools, SSR support, HMR and testability that provide/inject does not offer.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Implicit coupling.</strong> A component that injects has an invisible requirement on an ancestor. Throwing a clear error beats a mysterious <code>undefined</code> three components away.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the nearest provider wins.</strong> Usually the feature you want, occasionally the reason a value is not what you expected.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Provide/inject across sibling components.</strong> It only flows downward; siblings need a shared ancestor or a store.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use <code>Symbol</code> keys exported from one module, typed with <code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> in TypeScript.</li>`,
        `<li>Provide <code>readonly()</code> state and explicit mutators, so writes stay traceable.</li>`,
        `<li>Wrap the pair in <code>provideX()</code>/<code>useX()</code> composables — consumers get a typed function and never see a key.</li>`,
        `<li>Throw a descriptive error when a required injection is missing; silent <code>undefined</code> is the worst outcome.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for it at the right scale: two or three levels of drilling is fine and more explicit; provide/inject shines for component-library context (a <code>&lt;Tabs&gt;</code> talking to its <code>&lt;Tab&gt;</code> children) and app-wide configuration.</li>`,
        `<li>Use Pinia for shared application state — provide/inject is dependency injection, not state management.</li>`,
        `<li>Document the contract in the provider so the implicit dependency is at least written down somewhere.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What problem does provide/inject solve?"</strong><br>A: Prop drilling. When a deeply nested component needs data from a distant ancestor, props force every intermediate component to declare and forward something it does not use — noisy, and it breaks whenever the tree changes. An ancestor provides a value under a key and any descendant injects it directly. Vue implements it by having each instance's <code>provides</code> object inherit prototypally from its parent's, so <code>inject</code> walks up the chain and the nearest provider wins.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is injected data reactive?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Not by itself. The mechanism just passes a value down the tree; reactivity is a property of what you pass. Provide a <code>ref</code>, <code>reactive</code> object or <code>computed</code> and descendants track changes normally. Provide a primitive — or accidentally provide <code>myRef.value</code> — and they get a frozen snapshot with no error to tell you.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "provide/inject or Pinia?"</strong><br>A: provide/inject is dependency injection scoped to a component subtree; Pinia is application state management. I use provide/inject for context that belongs to a tree — a <code>&lt;Tabs&gt;</code> component sharing state with its <code>&lt;Tab&gt;</code> children, a theme, a form context, an injected API client — and Pinia for state the whole app shares, like auth or a cart, where devtools, SSR support, HMR and easy testing matter.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must inject be called during setup?"</strong><br>A: Because it resolves against the currently rendering component instance, which Vue only sets while setup runs. In an event handler or after an <code>await</code>, that instance is gone, so the lookup returns <code>undefined</code>. Same constraint as lifecycle hooks — call it synchronously at the top of setup and use the value later.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you stop descendants mutating provided state?"</strong><br>A: Provide <code>readonly(state)</code> together with the functions that are allowed to change it. Descendants can read freely and must call a mutator to write, so every change goes through one auditable place. Without that, provided reactive state is effectively a global mutable variable and you lose the traceability the props/emits model exists to give you.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>provide(key, value)</code> in an ancestor · <code>inject(key, default?)</code> in any descendant.</li>`,
        `<li>Solves <strong>prop drilling</strong>; flows downward only (no siblings).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not reactive by itself</strong> — provide a <code>ref</code>/<code>reactive</code>/<code>computed</code>, never <code>.value</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Must be called <strong>synchronously in setup</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Nearest provider wins (prototype-chain lookup) ⇒ subtree overrides work.</li>`,
        `<li><code>Symbol</code> keys + <code>InjectionKey&lt;T&gt;</code> to avoid collisions and get types.</li>`,
        `<li><code>readonly()</code> state + mutator functions keeps writes traceable.</li>`,
        `<li><code>app.provide()</code> for app-wide values; <code>inject(key, fallback, true)</code> for factory defaults.</li>`,
        `<li>Subtree context → provide/inject. App-wide state → Pinia.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Component Registration, Dynamic Components and refs',
      slug: 'registration-dynamic-components',
      order: 4,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>This topic collects the three "how do I actually reach a component?" questions that come up constantly in real work and in interviews: how does Vue know what <code>&lt;UserCard&gt;</code> refers to, how do you render a component whose identity is decided at runtime, and how do you call a method on a child when props and events are not enough.</p>`,
        `<p>Each has a failure mode worth knowing. Global registration quietly defeats tree-shaking. Dynamic components without <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> destroy state users expected to keep. And template refs are the escape hatch that, over-used, turns a declarative app back into jQuery — so interviewers listen carefully for whether you know when <em>not</em> to reach for one.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Registration</strong> is how a name in a template resolves to a component definition. Two options:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Local</strong> — in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, importing a component <em>is</em> registering it. The import is a top-level binding, so the template can use it. This is the default and the right choice.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Global</strong> — <code>app.component('BaseButton', BaseButton)</code> makes it usable in every template without an import. Convenient for a handful of design-system primitives, harmful as a habit.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Dynamic components</strong> — <code>&lt;component :is="x" /&gt;</code> — render whatever <code>x</code> currently is: a component object, a registered name, or even an HTML tag string. This is how tab interfaces, wizards and CMS-driven block renderers are built without a chain of <code>v-if</code>s.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Template refs</strong> — <code>ref="name"</code> — give you a direct handle on a DOM element or a child component instance, for the cases the declarative model does not cover: focusing an input, measuring an element, calling <code>play()</code> on a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>, or integrating a non-Vue library.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Registration and tree-shaking.</strong> A locally registered component is a normal import, so a bundler can see exactly what is used and drop the rest. A globally registered one is referenced by string at runtime, so the bundler must keep it whether or not any template uses it. Global registration also hides dependencies: reading a component file no longer tells you what it depends on.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> and component identity.</strong> When <code>is</code> changes, Vue unmounts the current component and mounts the new one — full teardown, full setup, state gone. That is usually correct, and occasionally exactly wrong: switching tabs and losing a half-filled form is a bug, not a feature. <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> caches the instances instead of destroying them, swapping <code>onUnmounted</code>/<code>onMounted</code> for the <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> pair.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch2-dynamic-components.svg|Diagram of component :is switching between three components, with and without KeepAlive, showing unmount-and-remount versus cached deactivate-and-activate|Without KeepAlive a dynamic component is destroyed and rebuilt on every switch; with it, instances are cached and merely deactivated.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Template refs and timing.</strong> In <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, declare <code>const el = ref(null)</code> and use <code>ref="el"</code> in the template; Vue assigns the element or component instance <em>after mount</em>. Before that it is <code>null</code> — which is why reading <code>el.value</code> at the top of setup always fails and why focus calls belong in <code>onMounted</code>. (Vue 3.5 adds <code>useTemplateRef()</code>, which decouples the variable name from the string used in the template.)</p>`,
        `<p>Refs inside <code>v-for</code> collect into an <strong>array</strong>, whose order is not guaranteed to match the source array — if order matters, use a function ref and build the mapping yourself.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>What a component ref exposes.</strong> With <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, a component is <em>closed by default</em>: its internals are private and the parent's ref sees nothing useful. To expose something deliberately, call <code>defineExpose({ focus, reset })</code>. This is a genuine improvement over Vue 2, where a parent could reach into any child's data — the closed default forces components to have an intentional public API.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Local registration: the import IS the registration --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport UserCard from './UserCard.vue'\nimport { defineAsyncComponent } from 'vue'\n\n// Lazy: fetched only when first rendered — good for modals and heavy charts\nconst HeavyChart = defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./HeavyChart.vue'))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;UserCard :user="user" /&gt;\n  &lt;HeavyChart v-if="showChart" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// main.js — global registration, for genuine design-system primitives only\nimport BaseButton from './components/base/BaseButton.vue'\nimport BaseInput from './components/base/BaseInput.vue'\n\napp.component('BaseButton', BaseButton)\napp.component('BaseInput', BaseInput)\n// Every template can now use &lt;BaseButton&gt; with no import —\n// at the cost of tree-shaking and explicit dependencies.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Dynamic components — a tab interface that keeps its state:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, shallowRef } from 'vue'\nimport ProfileTab from './ProfileTab.vue'\nimport SettingsTab from './SettingsTab.vue'\nimport BillingTab from './BillingTab.vue'\n\nconst tabs = { profile: ProfileTab, settings: SettingsTab, billing: BillingTab }\nconst current = ref('profile')\n\n// ⚠️ storing a component in a plain ref makes it deeply reactive for no reason.\n// shallowRef avoids proxying the whole component definition.\nconst activeComponent = shallowRef(ProfileTab)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;nav&gt;\n    &lt;button v-for="(_, name) in tabs" :key="name"\n            :class="{ active: current === name }"\n            @click="current = name"&gt;\n      {{ name }}\n    &lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/nav&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- KeepAlive caches instances: a half-filled form survives a tab switch --&gt;\n  &lt;KeepAlive :max="5"&gt;\n    &lt;component :is="tabs[current]" :user-id="userId" @saved="onSaved" /&gt;\n  &lt;/KeepAlive&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- :is also accepts a plain tag name — useful for polymorphic components --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\ndefineProps({ as: { type: String, default: 'div' } })   // 'h1' | 'section' | 'div'\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;component :is="as"&gt;&lt;slot /&gt;&lt;/component&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Template refs — DOM elements, lists, and a child's exposed API:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onMounted, nextTick } from 'vue'\n\nconst inputEl = ref(null)        // name must match ref="inputEl"\nconst itemEls = ref([])          // v-for refs collect into an array\nconst modal = ref(null)          // a child COMPONENT instance\n\nonMounted(() =&gt; {\n  inputEl.value.focus()          // available now, null before mount\n})\n\nasync function addAndScroll() {\n  items.value.push(newItem)\n  await nextTick()               // wait for the DOM to catch up\n  itemEls.value.at(-1)?.scrollIntoView()\n}\n\nfunction openModal() {\n  modal.value.open()             // only works because Modal called defineExpose\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input ref="inputEl"&gt;\n  &lt;li v-for="item in items" :key="item.id" ref="itemEls"&gt;{{ item.name }}&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;Modal ref="modal" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Modal.vue — closed by default; expose an intentional public API --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref } from 'vue'\n\nconst isOpen = ref(false)                 // private\nfunction open()  { isOpen.value = true }\nfunction close() { isOpen.value = false }\n\n// Without this, the parent's ref gives access to nothing.\ndefineExpose({ open, close })\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Registering everything globally.</strong> Kills tree-shaking, hides each file's dependencies, and makes "where does this component come from?" unanswerable by reading the file.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading a template ref during setup.</strong> It is <code>null</code> until mount. Use <code>onMounted</code>, or <code>await nextTick()</code> after a change that creates the element.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting a component ref to expose everything.</strong> <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> components are closed; without <code>defineExpose</code> the parent sees nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>v-for</code> refs are in source order.</strong> The array order is not guaranteed to match; use a function ref if you need a reliable mapping.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Losing state on tab switches.</strong> <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> unmounts the outgoing component. Wrap in <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> when state should survive — and remember that <code>onMounted</code> no longer re-runs, so per-visit work belongs in <code>onActivated</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Unbounded <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code>.</strong> Caching every component ever visited is a memory leak with good intentions. Use <code>:max</code>, or <code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Storing a component in a deep <code>ref</code>.</strong> Vue will proxy the entire component definition. Use <code>shallowRef</code> — or <code>markRaw</code> — for component objects.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using refs to do what props and events already do.</strong> Reaching into a child to set a value is imperative code in a declarative framework; it breaks one-way flow and is the main thing interviewers are checking for here.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Register locally by default. Reserve global registration for a small, stable set of primitives used nearly everywhere.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>defineAsyncComponent</code> for anything heavy and conditionally rendered — modals, editors, charts — so it leaves the initial bundle.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> over long <code>v-if</code>/<code>v-else-if</code> chains once there are more than two branches; it turns a conditional into a lookup table.</li>`,
        `<li>Wrap dynamic components in <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> when losing state would surprise the user, and always bound it with <code>:max</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat template refs as a last resort, for genuinely imperative APIs: focus, scroll, measure, media playback, third-party library mounting.</li>`,
        `<li>Expose the smallest possible surface with <code>defineExpose</code> — methods, not raw state — so parents cannot depend on internals.</li>`,
        `<li>Type component refs in TypeScript with <code>ref&lt;InstanceType&lt;typeof Modal&gt; | null&gt;(null)</code> so exposed methods are checked.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Global vs local component registration?"</strong><br>A: Local registration — in <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>, simply importing the component — keeps dependencies explicit and lets the bundler tree-shake, because usage is statically visible. Global registration via <code>app.component()</code> removes the import everywhere but resolves by string at runtime, so the bundler must keep the component whether or not it is used, and a file no longer documents what it depends on. I register globally only for a few design-system primitives.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you render a component chosen at runtime?"</strong><br>A: <code>&lt;component :is="x" /&gt;</code>, where <code>x</code> is a component object, a registered name or an HTML tag string. It replaces long <code>v-if</code> chains for tabs, wizards and CMS block renderers. Store the component in a <code>shallowRef</code> rather than a deep <code>ref</code> so Vue does not proxy the whole definition.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> do and what changes when you add it?"</strong><br>A: It caches component instances instead of destroying them on switch, so state and scroll position survive. The lifecycle changes: <code>onMounted</code>/<code>onUnmounted</code> stop firing on each switch and you get <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> instead, so anything that must run per visit — refetching, restarting a timer — moves there. Bound it with <code>:max</code> or <code>include</code>/<code>exclude</code>, because caching everything is a memory leak.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is my template ref null?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Refs are populated after the component mounts. Reading one during setup — the synchronous body — always gives <code>null</code>. Read it in <code>onMounted</code>, or after <code>await nextTick()</code> if the element was just created by a state change. If the element is inside a <code>v-if</code> that is currently false, it does not exist at all.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can a parent call a child's method?"</strong><br>A: Only what the child chooses to expose. In Vue 3, <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> components are closed by default, so a template ref on the child gives the parent nothing until the child calls <code>defineExpose({ open, close })</code>. That is deliberate — it forces an intentional public API instead of Vue 2's free access to any child's internals. And it should be the exception: props and events cover almost every case, and reaching into children is how you end up with untraceable state.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>: <strong>import = register</strong>. Global via <code>app.component()</code> costs tree-shaking.</li>`,
        `<li><code>defineAsyncComponent(() =&gt; import('./X.vue'))</code> for lazy, heavy components.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;component :is="x" /&gt;</code> — component object, registered name, or tag string.</li>`,
        `<li>Store components in <code>shallowRef</code> / <code>markRaw</code>, never a deep <code>ref</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;KeepAlive&gt;</code> caches instead of destroying → <code>onActivated</code>/<code>onDeactivated</code> replace mount/unmount. Bound with <code>:max</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Template ref: <code>const el = ref(null)</code> + <code>ref="el"</code>; populated <strong>after mount</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-for</code> refs → array, order not guaranteed.</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> components are <strong>closed</strong> — <code>defineExpose()</code> to publish an API.</li>`,
        `<li>Refs are an escape hatch: focus, scroll, measure, third-party libs. Not a substitute for props/emits.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
